	<article class="subpost" aria-labelledby="goscouting-label" id="goscouting">
		{{ websiteCarousel([
			{ url: "https://web.archive.org/web/20260723061613if_/https://www.goscouting.org/", alt: "The Go Scouting homepage, with a mountain-and-forest banner reading \"Preparing Kids For Real Life\" beside a ZIP code search bar to find local programs, and three \"About the Program\" cards below answering what a child does, what they gain, and whether Scouting is right for their family." }
		]) }}
		<h3 id="goscouting-label">Go Scouting</h3>
		<h4><a href="https://www.goscouting.org/" rel="external" target="_blank">Website</a></h4>
		<p>A recruitment and marketing website for Northern Star Scouting, the local Scouting council serving Minnesota and western Wisconsin. It replaced JoinCubs.org (which now redirects here) to instead cover all of Northern Star's programs: Cub Scouts, Scouts BSA, Venturing, and Exploring, in place of just Cub Scouting. It formerly offered unit name and school district based searching alongside ZIP code lookup, but now only has a simple ZIP code lookup to help families find a program near them.</p>
		<p>The most technically interesting of the retired search methods was the ZIP code lookup, which was powered by a precomputed neighbor map rather than a live geocoding query. For every ZIP code in the council's territory, a custom algorithm found its geographic center and drew a 25-mile radius around it; any other ZIP code whose center fell inside that circle was recorded as a neighbor (so a search for 55111 would return every other ZIP code within 25 miles of its center). Building that map included checks to avoid re-processing ZIP codes already accounted for, to stay within the council's boundary, and to adjust the radius as ZIP code density changed, since a rural ZIP code covers more ground and its residents can travel further in the same time it takes to cross a dense one like downtown Minneapolis.</p>
		<h4>Technologies</h4>
		<p>GoScouting.org is built the same way as this site: Eleventy (11ty) generates the static pages from hand-written Nunjucks templates and plain CSS/JavaScript, with no client-side framework, deployed to Cloudflare Workers. As it has a private GitHub repository, there is no source code available.</p>
