	<article class="subpost" aria-labelledby="scoutingplantitforward-label" id="scoutingplantitforward">
		{{ websiteCarousel([
			{ url: "https://web.archive.org/web/20260818005234if_/https://scoutingplantitforward.org/", alt: "The Scouting Plant It Forward homepage, with a forest photo background reading \"Plant It Forward. Support a Scout. Create a Legacy.\" next to a stat reading \"Because of you, 787 Trees Planted,\" followed by the start of a \"Why Donate?\" section explaining that donations fund trees planted in the Superior National Forest through the Arbor Day Foundation." }
		]) }}
		<h3 id="scoutingplantitforward-label">Scouting Plant It Forward</h3>
		<h4><a href="https://scoutingplantitforward.org/" rel="external" target="_blank">Website</a></h4>
		<p>A fundraising website for Northern Star Scouting, offering an alternative to the traditional popcorn sale: instead of a product, donors fund a native tree planted in the Superior National Forest through the Arbor Day Foundation, keeping the benefit local to the community that donates. Proceeds are split evenly between the participating Scout and the council, which covers card-processing fees and tree-planting logistics.</p>
		<h4>Technologies</h4>
		<p>ScoutingPlantItForward.org starts from the same Eleventy (11ty) static build as this site, but layers a Cloudflare Workers backend on top to handle logins, payments, and reporting:</p>
		<ul>
			<li>Payments run through Clover's iframe integration, so card details are entered directly into a Clover-hosted frame and never touch this site's servers &mdash; it only ever receives a payment token back, keeping the PCI DSS burden much lower. Cloudflare Turnstile guards the donate and checkout forms after Clover's own bot protection didn't work correctly.</li>
			<li>A full account system backs donor and Scout logins: Cloudflare D1 stores users, troops, and transactions, while Cloudflare KV stores sessions, checked on every page load so a stolen or edited cookie alone can't forge a session. Passwords are salted and hashed with PBKDF2-SHA256, and forgotten-password requests hand off to an n8n webhook to email a reset link.</li>
			<li>Every page is deliberately named <code>template.html</code> instead of <code>index.html</code>. Cloudflare Workers with static assets skips running the Worker entirely whenever a request matches an existing static file (for speed and to save on Worker invocations), so forcing a 404 on the "pretty" URL is what lets the Worker step in to inject login state or dashboard data, while every other request still gets served straight from the fast static cache.</li>
			<li>An n8n workflow triggers a Cloudflare deploy hook every night, rebuilding the site so data like the homepage's "trees planted" counter stays current even on days with no code changes.</li>
		</ul>
		<p>It has a private GitHub repository.</p>
